\paragraph{(F1)} The leading-term surrogate
$\Lead(u)=\exp(-u(\log u+\log\log u-1))$ evaluates to $\Lead(3)\approx 0.561$
where the true value is $\rho(3)\approx0.0487$ --- a twelvefold overshoot,
persisting through $u\approx6$, i.e.\ throughout the range a toy experiment can
reach.

\paragraph{(F2)} The smoothness of $x^2-N$ is not the smoothness of a random
integer at toy scale: even against correctly integrated $\rho$ the ratios were
non-monotone, the suspected mechanism being the quadratic-character constraint
on the prime divisors of $x^2-N$.

\section{A Dickman envelope class}

The Dickman function is the continuous solution of $\rho\equiv1$ on $[0,1]$ and
$u\rho'(u)=-\rho(u-1)$ for $u>1$; equivalently
\begin{equation}\label{eq:delay}
u\,\rho(u)\;=\;\int_{u-1}^{u}\rho(t)\,dt\qquad(u\ge1).
\end{equation}
Its closed form is known only on $[0,1]$ ($\rho=1$) and $(1,2]$
($\rho(u)=1-\log u$). All tail arguments below use \eqref{eq:delay} in one
direction only, so we work with an envelope class.

\begin{definition}[Dickman majorant]
A function $\rho:\R\to\R$ is a \emph{Dickman majorant} if
(i) $\rho\ge0$; (ii) $\rho(u)=1$ for $0\le u\le1$; (iii) $\rho$ is
non-increasing on $[0,\infty)$; (iv) $u\rho(u)\le\int_{u-1}^{u}\rho(t)\,dt$ for
all $u\ge1$.
\end{definition}

The true Dickman function satisfies (i)--(iii) classically and (iv) with
equality, hence is a majorant; every bound below therefore applies to it.

\begin{proposition}[Non-vacuity]
The indicator $\rho_0(u)=\mathbf 1[u\le1]$ is a Dickman majorant and satisfies
the auxiliary normalisation $\rho_0(2)\le 1-\log 2$ used in Section~3.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Clauses (i)--(iii) follow from the case split on $u\le1$. For (iv): if $u>1$ the
left side vanishes and the integrand is non-negative; if $u=1$ the integrand is
identically $1$ on $[0,1]$, so both sides equal $1$ --- the inequality is tight
exactly where it must be. Finally $\rho_0(2)=0\le1-\log2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Integrability]
A Dickman majorant is interval-integrable on every $[a,b]\subseteq[0,\infty)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A monotone function on a compact interval is integrable; clause (iii) supplies
monotonicity on every subinterval of $[0,\infty)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Contraction step]\label{thm:step}
For every Dickman majorant $\rho$ and every $u\ge1$,
\[\rho(u)\;\le\;\frac{\rho(u-1)}{u}.\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By antitonicity $\rho(t)\le\rho(u-1)$ on $[u-1,u]$, an interval of length $1$,
so $\int_{u-1}^{u}\rho\le\rho(u-1)$. Combined with clause (iv) this gives
$u\rho(u)\le\rho(u-1)$; divide by $u\ge1>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Factorial tail at integers]\label{thm:fact}
For every Dickman majorant and every $n\in\N$, $\rho(n)\le1/n!$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction: $\rho(0)=1=1/0!$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:step} at $u=n+1$ gives
$\rho(n+1)\le\rho(n)/(n+1)\le1/(n+1)!$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Factorial tail, real argument]\label{thm:factreal}
For every Dickman majorant and every $u\ge0$,
$\rho(u)\le 1/\lfloor u\rfloor!$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\lfloor u\rfloor\le u$ and $\rho$ is non-increasing, so
$\rho(u)\le\rho(\lfloor u\rfloor)$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:fact}.
\end{proof}

\section{The leading-term surrogate overshoots}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{leading-term Dickman surrogate} is
$\Lead(u)=\exp(-u(\log u+\log\log u-1))$ for $u>e$.
\end{definition}

The asymptotic statement $\rho(u)=\Lead(u)^{1+o(1)}$ is classical. What is not
correct --- and is routinely assumed --- is that $\Lead(u)\approx\rho(u)$
numerically in the computationally accessible range.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:log3}
$\log 3<1.1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
From $1.01<e^{0.01}$ raise to the tenth power: $e^{0.1}>1.01^{10}>1.1046$.
Multiplying by $e>2.7182818$ gives $e^{1.1}>3$; apply $\log$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:leadnum}
$\Lead(3)>0.54$ and $\Lead(4)>0.038$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $\Lead(3)$: $1<\log3<1.1$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:log3}, and
$\log\log3\le\log3-1<0.1$ by $\log x\le x-1$; hence the exponent exceeds
$-3(1.1+0.1-1)=-0.6$ and $\Lead(3)>e^{-0.6}=(e^{-0.05})^{12}\ge0.95^{12}>0.54$.
For $\Lead(4)$: $\log4=2\log2\in(1.386,1.387)$, so $\log\log4<0.387$, the
exponent exceeds $-3.1$, and
$e^{-3.1}=(e^{-0.1})^{31}\ge0.9^{31}>0.038$.
\end{proof}

We carry the classical band value $\rho(2)=1-\log2$ as a hypothesis so that all
statements stay inside the majorant class; the explicit witness satisfies it, so
nothing is vacuous.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:rho34}
If $\rho$ is a Dickman majorant with $\rho(2)\le1-\log2$ then
\[\rho(3)\le\tfrac{1-\log2}{3}\approx0.1023,\qquad
\rho(4)\le\tfrac{1-\log2}{12}\approx0.0256.\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Two applications of Theorem~\ref{thm:step}, at $u=3$ and $u=4$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Leading-term overshoot at $u=3$]
For every Dickman majorant with $\rho(2)\le1-\log2$,
\[\Lead(3)\;>\;5\,\rho(3).\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$5\rho(3)\le5(1-\log2)/3<0.5115$ using $\log2>0.6931471803$, while
$\Lead(3)>0.54$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:leadnum}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Persistence at $u=4$]
Under the same hypotheses, $\Lead(4)>\rho(4)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\rho(4)\le(1-\log2)/12<0.0256$ while $\Lead(4)>0.038$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The certified factor at $u=3$ is $5$; the measured factor is $12$. The gap is
slack in the tail bound $\rho(3)\le0.1023$ against the true $0.0487$ --- a
factor $2.1$, which is remarkable for an argument that never constructs $\rho$.
\end{remark}

\begin{lemma}[Elementary Stirling]\label{lem:stirling}
For every $n\in\N$, $n^{n}\le n!\,e^{n}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction. Write $(n+1)^n=n^n(1+1/n)^n$ and use $(1+1/n)^n\le e$, which follows
from $1+x\le e^x$ at $x=1/n$ raised to the $n$-th power; then
$(n+1)^n\le n!\,e^{n+1}$, and multiplying by $n+1$ gives
$(n+1)^{n+1}\le(n+1)!\,e^{n+1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Shape bound]\label{thm:shape}
For every Dickman majorant and every $u\ge1$, with $n=\lfloor u\rfloor\ge1$,
\[\rho(u)\;\le\;\Big(\frac{e}{n}\Big)^{n}\;=\;\exp\big(-n(\log n-1)\big).\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:factreal} gives $\rho(u)\le1/n!$ and Lemma~\ref{lem:stirling}
gives $1/n!\le e^{n}/n^{n}$; rewrite $n^{n}=\exp(n\log n)$.
\end{proof}

Thus the $u^{-u}$ decay encoded by $\Lead$ is rigorously valid; only the
numerical calibration, including the $\log\log u$ refinement, fails at small
$u$.

\section{An unconditional sub-exponential cost floor}

\subsection{The model}

Consider an idealized sieve acting on values of size $e^{L}$ with factor base
the primes up to $B=e^{b}$. It needs on the order of $\pi(B)\approx e^{b}$
relations, and each candidate succeeds with probability of order $\rho(u)$,
$u=L/b$. The cost is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:cost}
C(b)\;=\;\frac{e^{b}}{\rho(L/b)}.
\end{equation}
Classical analysis balances the two forces at $b\approx\sqrt L$ and obtains
$L[1/2]$. We prove the corresponding lower bound unconditionally and uniformly
in $b$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:twopow}
For every $n\in\N$, $2^{n}\le(n+1)!$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction: $2^{n+1}=2\cdot2^{n}\le(n+2)2^{n}\le(n+2)(n+1)!=(n+2)!$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[AM--GM in cost form]\label{lem:amgm}
For $b>0$ and $c\ge0$, $\;2\sqrt c\le b+c/b$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$b+c/b-2\sqrt c=(b-\sqrt c)^2/b\ge0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Model floor]\label{lem:modelfloor}
For $L\ge0$ and $b>0$,
\[2\sqrt{L\log2}-2\log2\;\le\;b+\Big(\frac{L}{b}-2\Big)\log2.\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Lemma~\ref{lem:amgm} with $c=L\log2$, noting $c/b=(L/b)\log2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sub-exponential cost floor]\label{thm:floor}
Let $\rho$ be any Dickman majorant and let $0<b\le L$ with $\rho(L/b)>0$. Then
\[\frac{e^{b}}{\rho(L/b)}\;\ge\;\exp\big(2\sqrt{L\log2}-2\log2\big).\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Put $u=L/b\ge1$, $n=\lfloor u\rfloor=m+1\ge1$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:factreal}
and Lemma~\ref{lem:twopow}, $\rho(u)\le1/n!\le2^{-m}$. Since $u<n+1=m+2$ we have
$u-2<m$, so $2^{\,u-2}\le 2^{m}$ and hence
$\rho(u)\,e^{(u-2)\log2}\le1$. Multiplying by $e^{b}$ and dividing by
$\rho(u)>0$ gives $e^{b}/\rho(u)\ge e^{\,b+(u-2)\log2}$, and
Lemma~\ref{lem:modelfloor} bounds the exponent below by
$2\sqrt{L\log2}-2\log2$.
\end{proof}

With $L=\log N$ this reads
$C\ge\exp(2\sqrt{\log2}\sqrt{\log N}-2\log2)$: the $\sqrt{\log N}$ is exactly
the $L[1/2]$ signature that the toy-scale sweep failed to resolve, obtained here
without measurement, without asymptotics, and uniformly over every admissible
factor base.

\subsection{The sharpened floor via the Legendre transform}

Theorem~\ref{thm:floor} spends the factorial on $n!\ge2^{n-1}$, which loses the
$\log\log$ factor of the classical exponent. Replacing it by $n!\ge(n/e)^{n}$
(Lemma~\ref{lem:stirling}) turns the cost exponent into
$E(b)=b+u(\log u-1)$ with $u=L/b$, whose infimum has no closed form. The
Legendre transform supplies a family of closed-form floors.

\begin{lemma}[Young/Legendre inequality for $x\log x$]\label{lem:young}
For $u>0$ and any $\ell\in\R$,
\[\ell u-e^{\ell}\;\le\;u(\log u-1),\]
with equality at $u=e^{\ell}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Substituting $t=ue^{-\ell}>0$ reduces the claim to $t-1\le t\log t$, which is
$\log s\le s-1$ at $s=1/t$ multiplied by $t>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpened model floor]\label{thm:sharp}
For all $L>0$, $b>0$ and $\ell\ge0$,
\[2\sqrt{\ell L}-e^{\ell}\;\le\;b+\frac{L}{b}\Big(\log\frac{L}{b}-1\Big).\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Lemma~\ref{lem:young} with $u=L/b$ gives
$\ell L/b-e^{\ell}\le(L/b)(\log(L/b)-1)$; Lemma~\ref{lem:amgm} with $c=\ell L$
gives $2\sqrt{\ell L}\le b+\ell L/b$. Add.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The $\sqrt{L\log L}$ shape]\label{thm:sqrtlog}
For $L\ge1$ and $b>0$,
\[\sqrt{2L\log L}-\sqrt L\;\le\;b+\frac{L}{b}\Big(\log\frac{L}{b}-1\Big).\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $\ell=\tfrac12\log L\ge0$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}: then $e^{\ell}=\sqrt
L$ and $2\sqrt{\ell L}=\sqrt{2L\log L}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Each floor in Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} is attained at $u=e^{\ell}$, so the
supremum over $\ell$ recovers the exact infimum of the smooth model, and
$\ell=\tfrac12\log L$ is asymptotically optimal up to lower-order terms.
Transporting Theorem~\ref{thm:sqrtlog} back to a statement about $\rho$ requires
replacing $u$ by $\lfloor u\rfloor$ inside the Stirling bound, costing an $O(1)$
additive shift in the exponent; that bookkeeping is left open, while the $2^{n}$
version, Theorem~\ref{thm:floor}, is already unconditional.
\end{remark}

\section{The quadratic-sieve pool is not a random pool --- and exactly where}

\subsection{The hit pattern}

A prime $p$ divides $v(x)=x^2-N$ exactly when $x^2\equiv N\pmod p$: for
$p\nmid2N$, either $N$ is a quadratic residue and there are two roots per
period, or it is a non-residue and there are none. A random integer sequence is
divisible by $p$ once per period.

\begin{definition}[Hit pattern]
For an odd prime $p$ and $a\in\Z/p$, put
$h_p(a)=\#\{x\in\Z/p:x^2=a\}$, the number of hits of $p$ per period in the
sequence $x^2-N$ when $N\equiv a\pmod p$.
\end{definition}

The random-integer null model is the constant pattern $h\equiv1$.

\begin{theorem}[First moment]\label{thm:first}
For every prime $p$, $\sum_{a\in\Z/p}h_p(a)=p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The root sets $\{x:x^2=a\}$ are the fibres of the squaring map and partition
$\Z/p$, so their cardinalities sum to $p$.
\end{proof}

Thus the pool is indistinguishable from random \emph{on average} --- which is
precisely why first-moment analyses cannot account for the observed scatter.

\begin{lemma}[Character formula]\label{lem:char}
For an odd prime $p$ and $a\in\Z/p$, $\;h_p(a)=\chi_p(a)+1$, where $\chi_p$ is
the quadratic character with $\chi_p(0)=0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The standard root count for a quadratic: two roots for a nonzero square, none
for a non-residue, and the single root $x=0$ when $a=0$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Zero-lag dispersion never decays}

\begin{theorem}[Zero-lag dispersion identity]\label{thm:disp}
For every odd prime $p$,
\[\sum_{a\in\Z/p}\big(h_p(a)-1\big)^2\;=\;p-1.\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:char} the summand is $\chi_p(a)^2$, which is $1$ for $a\ne0$
and $0$ at $a=0$; summing the indicator of $\{a\ne0\}$ gives $p-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Second moment]\label{cor:second}
$\sum_a h_p(a)^2=2p-1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand $\sum_a(h_p(a)-1)^2=\sum_a h_p(a)^2-2\sum_a h_p(a)+p$ and use
Theorems~\ref{thm:first} and~\ref{thm:disp}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Normalised dispersion]
The mean square deviation of the hit pattern from the random model is exactly
$\frac1p\sum_a(h_p(a)-1)^2=1-\frac1p$, which is at least $1/2$ for every odd
prime and tends to $1$.
\end{corollary}

This is the formal content of ``the $O(1)$ corrections do not stabilise''. The
pool is maximally dispersed at every prime, forever; toy-scale smoothness ratios
inherit this fluctuation, which is the algebraic explanation of the non-monotone
ratios $0.26$--$9.3$ of Section~1.

\subsection{Nonzero lags are exactly random}

\begin{lemma}[Difference-of-squares count]\label{lem:diff}
Let $p$ be an odd prime and $c\in\Z/p$, $c\ne0$. Then
\[\#\{(x,y)\in(\Z/p)^2:y^2-x^2=c\}\;=\;p-1.\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Factor $y^2-x^2=(y-x)(y+x)$ and map $(x,y)\mapsto v=y-x$; since $c\ne0$ we have
$v\ne0$. Conversely, for $v\ne0$ the system $y-x=v$, $y+x=c/v$ has the unique
solution $x=(c/v-v)/2$, $y=(c/v+v)/2$ ($2$ is invertible as $p$ is odd), and it
satisfies $y^2-x^2=c$. The map is a bijection onto $(\Z/p)^{\times}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Pair correlation]\label{thm:pair}
For every odd prime $p$ and every $c\ne0$ in $\Z/p$,
\[\sum_{a\in\Z/p}h_p(a)\,h_p(a+c)\;=\;p-1.\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Fibre the solution set of $y^2-x^2=c$ over $a=x^2$. The fibre above $a$ is the
product of $\{x:x^2=a\}$ and $\{y:y^2=a+c\}$, of cardinality
$h_p(a)h_p(a+c)$. Sum over $a$ and apply Lemma~\ref{lem:diff}.
\end{proof}

Under the random model each term would be $1$, summing to $p$; the true value
$p-1$ differs only by the single degree of freedom removed by the zero residue,
and involves no character-sum cancellation or Weil-type error term at all.

\begin{corollary}[Autocorrelation dichotomy]\label{cor:dich}
Write $A(c)=\sum_a h_p(a)h_p(a+c)$. For every odd prime $p$ and every $c\ne0$,
\[A(0)-A(c)\;=\;(2p-1)-(p-1)\;=\;p.\]
\end{corollary}

The autocorrelation of the hit pattern is a perfect spike: constant $p-1$ off
the origin, $2p-1$ at the origin, the difference being exactly one full period.
All deviation from randomness sits at lag zero, and it is exactly quantified.

\subsection{Consequences for the smoothness heuristic}

On average the pool is exactly random (Theorem~\ref{thm:first}); in variance it
is maximally non-random with a deviation that never decays
(Theorem~\ref{thm:disp}); in all nonzero-lag correlations it is again exactly
random (Theorem~\ref{thm:pair}). Hence the substitution ``treat $x^2-N$ as a
random integer of its size'' is correct to first order and in all pair
correlations, but carries an irreducible per-prime variance $1-1/p$. At
asymptotic scales this variance is averaged over many primes and contributes
only to the $o(1)$ in the exponent; at toy scale, where a handful of primes
matter per value, it dominates --- which is the observed failure mode.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Numerical integration of $\rho$.}
Discretize $[0,u_{\max}]$ with uniform step $h$ such that $1/h\in\N$, set
$\rho\equiv1$ on $[0,1]$, and integrate $\rho(u+h)=\rho(u)-h\rho(u-1)/u$
forward; the delay value $\rho(u-1)$ is always on the grid. With
$h=5\times10^{-4}$ the scheme reproduces $\rho(2)=1-\log2$ to five digits, an
internal check. Cost: $O(u_{\max}/h)$.

\paragraph{Smoothness sampling for $x^2-N$.}
Draw $x$ uniformly in $[\lceil\sqrt N\rceil,\lfloor2\sqrt N\rfloor]$, form
$v=x^2-N$, strip all prime factors $\le B$ by trial division, and declare $v$
smooth if the cofactor is $1$; record $u=\log v/\log B$ from the actual $v$,
never from $N$. Cost: $O(\pi(B))$ divisions per sample.

\paragraph{Exact hit-pattern moments.}
For an odd prime $p$, tally $h_p(a)$ by squaring each residue, then form
$\sum_a(h_p(a)-1)^2$ and $A(c)$: $O(p)$ per lag in integer arithmetic.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What a null result bought}

The experiment set out to place the fourth stratum and could not: three strata
measured, one unmeasured. But the two facts that survived are stronger than the
measurement they replace. A successful measurement would have given a fitted
slope with error bars at one scale; instead we have a uniform lower bound on the
cost model at every scale and every factor-base choice
(Theorems~\ref{thm:floor} and~\ref{thm:sqrtlog}), and an exact identification of
the failure mode of the random-integer heuristic (Corollary~\ref{cor:dich}).

\subsection{Method ledger}

Three errors are recorded because the corrections are part of the result.
(i) The first design mis-scaled $u$: values $x^2-N$ of size $\approx N^{1/2}$
were binned as though of size $N$, invalidating the entire first comparison; the
anomaly that exposed it was empirical densities \emph{above} prediction, which
is impossible under a correct null. (ii) A success verdict drafted before the
data contradicted it and was regenerated from the computed numbers. (iii) A
syntax error in the analysis script was caught by parsing the source before
execution. Only (i) affected a scientific claim, and it was caught before
publication.

\subsection{Relation to classical theory}

The classical asymptotic $\Psi(X,X^{1/u})\sim X\rho(u)$ is the basis of the
$L[1/2]$ heuristic, and our envelope results are not competitive with it
asymptotically: the majorant class yields only upper bounds, and
$1/\lfloor u\rfloor!$ is weaker than $u^{-u(1+o(1))}$ by a bounded exponential
factor. Their virtue is different: they are unconditional, elementary, effective
at small $u$ where the classical asymptotics are silent, and they suffice for
the cost floor, which is the statement of interest for complexity
stratification. Likewise Theorem~\ref{thm:pair} is a degenerate case of the
general principle that curves over $\mathbb F_p$ have $p+O(\sqrt p)$ points;
what is special at degree $2$ is that $y^2-x^2=c$ degenerates into a hyperbola
$uv=c$ with \emph{exactly} $p-1$ points, and this exactness is what makes
Corollary~\ref{cor:dich} clean.

\section{Future directions}

\subsection{Zero-lag concentration for higher-degree sieve pools}

For an irreducible $f$ of degree $d$ put
$h_{f,p}(a)=\#\{x\bmod p:f(x)\equiv a\}$. For $d=2$ we proved total dispersion
$p-1$ at lag $0$ and exactly the random value $p-1$ at every nonzero lag. The
lag-$c$ correlation counts points on $f(y)-f(x)=c$, which for $d=2$ degenerates
to a hyperbola $uv=c$ with exactly $p-1$ points, while for $d\ge3$ it is an
absolutely irreducible curve with $p+O(\sqrt p)$ points by Weil --- so the
dichotomy must blur at order $\sqrt p$. The $d=3$ deviation is the smallest new
phenomenon that cannot be obtained by the same bijection, and it is directly
relevant to the higher-degree pools of the number field sieve.

\subsection{From $2^{n}$ to $n!$: the sharp sub-exponential floor}

Theorem~\ref{thm:floor} uses $n!\ge2^{n-1}$, giving $\exp(2\sqrt{L\log2})$.
Using $n!\ge(n/e)^n$ the model floor becomes $\inf_b[b+u(\log u-1)]$ with
$u=L/b$, whose asymptotics are $(1+o(1))\cdot2\sqrt{L\log L}$ --- the correct
$L[1/2,1]$ constant. The infimum has no closed form but is pinned between two
explicit AM--GM-type bounds obtained by freezing $\log u$ at $\log\sqrt L$;
Theorems~\ref{thm:sharp} and~\ref{thm:sqrtlog} supply one side. Only the
two-sided estimate, plus the floor-function bookkeeping, remains, and it is pure
one-variable analysis.

\subsection{A constructed Dickman function}

Every tail bound here is stated for the majorant class, which is non-vacuous by
an explicit witness but does not yet contain a \emph{constructed} Dickman
function. One can build $\rho$ band by band on $[n,n+1]$ as an explicit iterated
integral of the previous band, each band inheriting continuity, positivity and
monotonicity from its predecessor. Completing this construction would upgrade
every ``for all majorants'' statement into a statement about $\rho$ with
equality in the delay relation.

\subsection{Production-scale measurement}

The measurement itself remains open. To separate $\sqrt L$ from a constant one
needs several octaves of $L$; to resolve $\rho(u)$ at $u\ge4$ one needs of order
$1/\rho(u)\approx10^{3}$--$10^{5}$ samples per bin. Both are feasible at
production scale, and a properly powered sweep would turn the cost floor of
Theorem~\ref{thm:floor} from a bound into a calibration.

